\begin{abstract}
Text-to-speech that reads raw text has no lexicon: a rare word is read as guessed. Remedies train a reading-and-accent channel on recorded speech or edit words one at a time from exemplars. We do neither. The frozen backbone reads a sentence containing a \emph{common} word it already says correctly, and its own output then serves as the teacher for the same sentence, with that word replaced by a tagged, accented reading; this training pair is the whole idea. On Sarashina2.2-TTS, screened raters at Fleiss' $\kappa=0.85$ hear the prescribed accent on $0.89$ of unseen words against $0.57$ for kana, which cannot express one; kana wins no pair; naturalness is not measurably hurt. Moved untuned to autoregressive, diffusion, and encoder--decoder backbones, it transfers reading, $0.25$ to $0.47$ above no edit on $319$ words, and on CosyVoice~2 accent on two words in three, but not on Irodori; the paper locates why.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keywords}
speech synthesis, pronunciation control, pitch accent, self-distillation, parameter-efficient
fine-tuning
\end{keywords}

