\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Conventional cascade text-to-speech (TTS) resolved pronunciation before synthesis: a grapheme-to-phoneme (G2P) front-end consulted a lexicon, and an unknown word was fixed with a lexicon entry. End-to-end TTS trained on raw text dropped that stage, and with it the ability to be \emph{told} how to say a word: a rare kanji, a proper noun, or a domain term is rendered as the model guesses, and the only recourse is to change the model.

For Japanese, the problem is not only which morae are produced but which of them carries the pitch accent. Accent is lexically specified, and getting it wrong is not merely unnatural: the listener searches the lexicon for a word that was never uttered, so the cost falls on recognition.

\noindent\textbf{Non-claims.} We claim no novelty for low-rank adaptation or for phoneme-mode tag tokens as such (E2~TTS marks phoneme spans with special tokens~\cite{eskimez2024e2tts}), and no language independence; we do not measure sentence-level character error rate (degeneration is measured instead, \S\ref{sec:guard}), and we do not target homophone disambiguation.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Control signal}
The target word is replaced, in the input text, by \texttt{<PHON\_START>}\allowbreak\,\emph{accented
katakana}\,\allowbreak\texttt{<PHON\_END>}. An apostrophe marks the mora after which the pitch falls (the accent nucleus); a slash marks an accent-phrase boundary; an unmarked reading is flat (\emph{heiban}). The notation follows JEITA IT-4006. Across backbones, only the tag spelling changes.

\subsection{Training}
What installs the channel is the training pair in \S\ref{sec:method}: the tagged student input paired with the base's own output for the plain carrier. The parameterization of the update is incidental: any adaptation that leaves the synthesis stack frozen would serve, up to full fine-tuning of the language model, and we claim nothing for low-rank adaptation. The instance evaluated here, throughout, is rank-16 LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} ($\alpha=64$) on the query, key, value, and output projections plus \emph{only the two embedding rows of the tag tokens}, with every other parameter frozen: under $0.5\%$ of parameters move, the flow/vocoder stack is never touched, and training takes about one hour on a single consumer GPU.


Whether the channel can become voice-specific depends on where the speaker enters relative to the adapter. On Sarashina and T5Gemma, no speaker enters training: the teacher is the base's own continuation of the plain carrier, and identity is supplied later by the frozen synthesis stack from a reference the adapter never saw. Irodori can also generate without a speaker condition, but its teachers were cycled over a bank of synthetic references. CosyVoice~2 has no such option: its LLM input concatenates a speaker embedding, the text, and the prompt's speech tokens, so every teacher carries a voice.

\textbf{Tag tokens.} Sarashina ships the two tag tokens; on CosyVoice~2 and Irodori, they are added to the tokenizer as two new embedding rows. T5Gemma-TTS's stock tokenizer splits the string \texttt{<PHON\_START>} into six subwords, which the decoder then speaks, so two of Gemma's reserved single-token \texttt{<unused\emph{N}>} rows serve as delimiters instead, with no vocabulary resize; the accent apostrophe is already a stock token.

\section{Deployment: register once, applied automatically}
\label{sec:deploy}

The deployed system maintains a \emph{registry} that maps a surface form to its accented reading --- either \{\emph{reading}, \emph{accent index}\} or the accented katakana written directly --- and applies it via a router at synthesis time.

The router runs the public front-end over the \emph{whole} sentence, matches each registered surface \emph{at morpheme boundaries}, and then

\begin{enumerate}\itemsep2pt
  \item wraps only the matched span with the tags, carrying that entry's accented reading;
  \item \textbf{absorbs a following suffix} into the wrapper when the front-end attaches one, so
        that the tag never abuts raw unconverted orthography;
  \item \textbf{falls back to the unedited base} when no registered surface aligns with a morpheme
        boundary, and records that the edit did not fire.
\end{enumerate}

Step~2 matters. In distillation, the tag always wrapped a whole front-end token, inflection included. If it instead closes inside one --- the stem tagged, its inflection left outside as raw orthography --- the model meets a sequence it never trained on, and in our runs it looped on repeated morae (\S\ref{sec:guard}). 

\textbf{A word is registered once, with its accent, and every later occurrence in any sentence is corrected automatically}, with no per-sentence markup, no retraining and, unlike per-word editors~\cite{singh2026sonoedit,singh2026flowedit}, no exemplar.

