SpeechSplice~\cite{kurihara2026speechsplice}, concurrent work, edits pronunciation on T5Gemma-TTS with no training by replacing encoder hidden states; the present method needs no such intervention point.


\subsection{What does zero recorded data cost, and when?}
\label{sec:reftab}
CosyVoice~2 is the one backbone with a released UtterTune adapter, trained on $15{,}097$ hand-corrected recorded pairs. On the $319$-item test half under $42$ voices the data-free adapter trails it by $0.107$ ($[-0.160,-0.056]$, $p=8\times10^{-5}$; Table~\ref{tab:reading}), and on accent by $0.30$ (\S\ref{sec:listening}).

\label{sec:kana}
Reading accuracy is not where this method wins (Table~\ref{tab:reading}, \S\ref{sec:reftab}); what none of it settles is accent, because kana cannot express one, and writing the accent into the kana does not help: to the frozen base, an apostrophe is punctuation, not a nucleus. Giving that notation a meaning is what the distillation does, and \S\ref{sec:listening} measures it.

\subsection{Listening evaluation}
\label{sec:listening}
Accent is judged by ear. Rater time goes to the two backbones where the recipe ran as designed and to the one with a recorded-data comparator; T5Gemma, whose channel was trained on the half of its teacher set that survived (\S\ref{sec:method}), is scored objectively only. Screened raters judge $473$ complete sentences, blind and in randomized order, each under one of $42$ held-out voices, fixed by hash, so each cell spans $27$--$31$ voices.

Accent realization is a correctness judgment, so ability is gated by a known-answer screening test rather than by credentials. Admission requires $18$ of $19$ known-answer items, a threshold fixed before recruitment; the first attempt is binding. Candidates work with Japanese pitch accent, either professionally or in training; every recruitment route takes the same test, every candidate is paid according to their score, and no rater is told which rendition comes from which system.

\subsection{Naturalness}
\label{sec:nat}
Following the critique of absolute rating scales~\cite{kirkland2023mospit,bailly2025hottopics}, listeners choose the more natural of the \emph{same sentence} with and without the edit, or no difference, on the easy set (\S\ref{sec:exp}; $197$ pairs, one held-out voice each), where the edit does not change the word heard.

