\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
A pronunciation and accent control channel can be installed into a frozen TTS backbone for the price of a word list and one GPU-hour, with no recorded speech: distilling the model against itself on words it already says correctly is enough for the control to reach words it never saw, and a registry applies it wherever a registered word appears.

Table~\ref{tab:summary} says where the channel is worth having. Reading transfers on all four, within $0.02$--$0.09$ of plain kana. Accent was judged on three: on Sarashina2.2-TTS, the tag delivers it where kana cannot ($0.89$ vs.\ $0.57$); on CosyVoice~2, it reaches two words in three, compared with the recorded-data adapter's $0.96$; on Irodori, it exceeds no edit. Naturalness is non-inferior on the backbone the recipe was developed on, and on none of the three was it moved to an untuned state. The mechanism is the point: a model that already produces the behavior can be taught to request it from its own output alone. Where the recipe was developed, the channel carries reading and accent at no cost; moved untuned, reading everywhere and part of the accent; the rest is next. \textbf{Limitations.} One language; four raters; one recipe moved untuned; uneven teacher takes.

\section*{Funding and conflicts of interest}
This work received no external or institutional funding. Compute was performed on the author's own hardware, and the listening studies were paid for by the author personally. The author declares that the method evaluated here is their own, which is why every reported accent judgment is made by raters admitted through a known-answer screening test, none of whom is told which rendition comes from which system, under a design fixed before any audio was heard. One panel rater was approached directly by the author; screening outcomes are reported by route (\S\ref{sec:listening}).

\section*{Compliance with ethical standards}
The listening studies involve non-invasive listening judgments on synthesized speech. No audio, biometric, or personally identifiable data is collected from participants; only their judgments are recorded. All participants receive a written description of the task, the use of the data, and the compensation, and give informed consent before taking part. Participation is voluntary and may be discontinued at any time without penalty; a discontinued session is still paid. Raters are recruited through a commercial crowdsourcing platform and, in one case, through a direct approach; every candidate takes the same qualification screening, and those who do not pass are compensated for it regardless. Raters are asked to pause every 25 minutes and may stop and resume at any time.

